  "content": {
    "id": "p49-q11",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "$kx^2-(2k-1)x+k=0$. Find the values of $k$ for which this equation has:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p49-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "No real solutions ($\\Delta<0$ for $k\\ne0$)",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k>\\frac14$.",
          "worked": "For $k\\ne0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=[-(2k-1)]^2-4(k)(k)\\\\\\Delta&=4k^2-4k+1-4k^2\\\\\\Delta&=1-4k\\end{align*}$$\nNo real solutions requires $\\Delta<0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1-4k&<0\\\\-4k&<-1\\\\k&>\\frac14\\end{align*}$$\nFor $k=0$, the equation becomes $x=0$, which has one real solution. Therefore $k>\\frac14$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p49-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "One real solution ($\\Delta=0$ for $k\\ne0$)",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k=0$ or $k=\\frac14$. At $k=0$, the equation is linear.",
          "worked": "For $k\\ne0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=[-(2k-1)]^2-4(k)(k)\\\\\\Delta&=4k^2-4k+1-4k^2\\\\\\Delta&=1-4k\\end{align*}$$\nExactly one real solution requires $\\Delta=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1-4k&=0\\\\k&=\\frac14\\end{align*}$$\nFor $k=0$, the equation becomes $x=0$, which also has exactly one real solution.\nTherefore $k=0$ or $k=\\frac14$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p49-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Two real solutions ($\\Delta>0$ for $k\\ne0$)",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k<\\frac14$, with $k\\ne0$. At $k=0$, there is only one solution.",
          "worked": "For $k\\ne0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=[-(2k-1)]^2-4(k)(k)\\\\\\Delta&=4k^2-4k+1-4k^2\\\\\\Delta&=1-4k\\end{align*}$$\nTwo real solutions requires $\\Delta>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1-4k&>0\\\\-4k&>-1\\\\k&<\\frac14\\end{align*}$$\nFor $k=0$, the equation becomes $x=0$ and has only one solution, so exclude $k=0$.\nTherefore $k<\\frac14$, with $k\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      }
    ]
  },
